ds, including studies of mobile banking. By decomposing the components of attitudes, subjective norms, and perceived behavioral control, Taylor's research offers a nuanced approach to predicting and analyzing user intentions and behaviors in adopting new technologies.", "TODD:Todd is a researcher and theorist known for his association with the Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior. He co-proposed this theory, which has been applied in various studies, including those focused on mobile banking. Todd's work in this area highlights his contribution to understanding and predicting human behavior in the context of technology adoption and usage. His research provides valuable insights into the factors that influence individuals' intentions and actions, particularly in the rapidly evolving field of mobile banking. Through his theoretical and practical contributions, Todd has significantly impacted the study of behavioral intentions and decision-making processes.", "DAVIS:Davis is a researcher known for the Technology Acceptance Model, which is used to study consumer behavior in mobile banking", "VENKATESH:Venkatesh is a prominent researcher and author known for his significant contributions to the field of technology acceptance and usage. He is particularly associated with the development of the Unified Theory of Acceptance and Use of Technology (UTAUT), a theoretical framework that has been widely applied in various studies, including those focused on mobile banking. Venkatesh's work in this area has been instrumental in understanding how individuals adopt and use technology, providing valuable insights that have influenced both academic research and practical applications in technology adoption. His association with UTAUT underscores his expertise and leadership in the field, making him a key figure in the study of technology acceptance.", "FISHBEIN:Fishbein is a renowned theorist recognized for his significant contributions to the field of behavioral science. He is best known for developing the Theory of Reasoned Action, a pivotal framework that has laid the groundwork for further theoretical advancements, including the Theory of Planned Behavior and the Technology Acceptance Model. The Theory of Reasoned Action, formulated by Fishbein, explores the relationship between attitudes, intentions, and behaviors, providing a comprehensive understanding of how individuals make decisions and act upon them. This theory has been instrumental in various domains, influencing research and applications in psychology, marketing, health communication, and technology adoption. Fishbein's work continues to be a cornerstone in understanding human behavior, offering valuable insights into the factors that drive decision-making processes.", "DEB:DEB is a researcher and author who has conducted empirical studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. Through their work, DEB has contributed to the understanding of how mobile banking is perceived and adopted by users. Their research provides valuable insights into the factors influencing the acceptance of mobile banking technologies, helping to inform both academic discourse and practical applications in the field.", "LOMO-DAVID:Lomo-David is a researcher and author who has conducted empirical studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. His work focuses on understanding how users perceive and adopt mobile banking technologies. Through his research, Lomo-David aims to provide insights into the factors that influence the acceptance and use of mobile banking services, contributing valuable knowledge to the field of financial technology and user behavior.", "GLAVEE-GEO:GLAVEE-GEO is a researcher and author known for conducting empirical studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. Through their work, GLAVEE-GEO has contributed to the understanding of how mobile banking is perceived and adopted by users. Their research provides valuable insights into the factors influencing the acceptance of mobile banking technologies, which is crucial for financial institutions and technology developers aiming to enhance user experience and increase adoption rates. By focusing on empirical research, GLAVEE-GEO ensures that their findings are grounded in real-world data, offering practical implications for the industry.", "KHASAWNEH:Khasawneh is a researcher and author prominently recognized for their empirical studies on the acceptance and adoption of mobile banking. Their work has been referenced in various contexts related to mobile banking research, highlighting their contribution to understanding how users perceive and accept mobile banking technologies. Through rigorous empirical research, Khasawneh has provided valuable insights into the factors influencing the adoption of mobile banking, making significant contributions to the field.", "IRSHAIDAT:Irshaidat is a researcher and author prominently recognized for their empirical studies on the acceptance and adoption of mobile banking. Their work has been referenced in various contexts related to the research on mobile banking, highlighting their significant contributions to understanding how mobile banking is perceived and utilized by users. Through rigorous empirical research, Irshaidat has provided valuable insights into the factors influencing the acceptance of mobile banking technologies, thereby contributing to the broader discourse on digital financial services.", "TRAN:TRAN is a researcher and author who has conducted empirical studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. Through their work, TRAN has contributed to the understanding of how mobile banking is perceived and adopted by users. Their research provides valuable insights into the factors influencing the acceptance of mobile banking technologies, which is crucial for financial institutions and technology developers aiming to enhance user experience and increase adoption rates. TRAN's studies are characterized by a rigorous empirical approach, ensuring that the findings are both reliable and applicable in real-world scenarios.", "CORNER:CORNER is a researcher and author who has conducted empirical studies on the acceptance of mobile banking. Through their work, CORNER has contributed to the understanding of how mobile banking is perceived and adopted by users. Their research provides valuable insights into the factors influencing the acceptance of mobile banking technologies, helping to inform both academic discourse and practical applications in the field.", "THEORY OF PLANNED BEHAVIOR:The Theory of Planned Behavior is a psychological theory that links beliefs and behavior, used to understand consumer acceptance of mobile banking", "DECOMPOSED THEORY OF PLANNED BEHAVIOR:The Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior (DTPB) is a model developed by Taylor and Todd as an extension of Ajzen's Theory of Planned Behavior (TPB). This model is designed to provide a more detailed understanding of the factors that influence human behavior by breaking down the components of the original TPB. Specifically, the DTPB identifies and elaborates on the factors that affect attitudes, subjective norms, and perceived behavioral control, which are the core components of the TPB. By doing so, it offers a more nuanced framework for analyzing how these elements contribute to the formation of behavioral intentions and subsequent actions.\n\nThe DTPB is particularly useful in the context of studying consumer behavior, especially in areas such as mobile banking. By applying this model, researchers and practitioners can gain insights into the specific beliefs and influences that drive consumer decisions in adopting and using mobile banking services. The model's detailed approach allows for a comprehensive analysis of the various psychological and social factors that impact consumer behavior, making it a valuable tool for understanding and predicting how individuals will behave in specific contexts.\n\nOverall, the Decomposed Theory of Planned Behavior provides a robust framework for examining the complexities of human behavior, offering a deeper exploration of the factors that shape attitudes, norms, and control perceptions. This makes it an essential model for researchers and professionals seeking to understand and influence consumer behavior in various domains, including the rapidly evolving field of mobile banking.", "TECHNOLOGY ACCEPTANCE MODEL:The Technology Acceptance Model (TAM) is a widely recognized framework designed to explain and predict the acceptance and use of information technology services. Central to this model are two primary factors: perceived usefulness and perceived ease of use. Perceived usefulness refers to the degree to which a person believes that using a particular system would enhance their job performance, while perceived ease of use pertains to the degree to which a person believes that using the system would be free of effort. These factors are crucial in determining how users come to accept and utilize technology.\n\nTAM has been extensively applied across various domains to understand user acceptance of technology. One notable application of the model is in the field of mobile banking studies. In this context, TAM helps researchers and practitioners comprehend how users decide to adopt mobile banking services, which are increasingly becoming an integral part of the financial services landscape. By focusing on the perceived benefits and the user-friendliness of mobile banking applications, TAM provides valuable insights into the factors that drive user adoption and continued use of these technologies.\n\nOverall, the Technology Acceptance Model serves as a foundational tool for researchers and practitioners aiming to enhance user engagement with new technologies by addressing the key determinants of technology acceptance.", "UNIFIED THEORY OF ACCEPTANCE AND USAGE OF TECHNOLOGY:The Unified Theory of Acceptance and Usage of Technology is a model that explains user intentions to use technology and subsequent usage behavior, used in mobile banking research"], "parameters": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small"}}, "key": "embeddings_c5c3d9dd5605c968e907331c54cf22d08aa0ada17473858998f60c3d897fb9f1_v2"}